A||||1||blakeinspace||Power Door Locks! At Last!!!||||10||
Z||000000||blakeinspace||12-31-2001||05:55 PM||||weekend project...<BR>operation:  fix power door locks for car alarm installation -- or -- enduring freedom<P>Have you got power locks that take 2-3 bumps to maybe work or just hold the activation switch down a full second+ to give the actuator enough time/juice to actually lock/unlock your door?  For TWO years I have tolerated this nonsense... finally i'd had enough!  2 years ago I took the door panels off and did the not so precise method of "over-white-lithium-grease-everything-and-wideband-spray-stuff-and-hope-that-the-overspray-and-or-grease-ricochet-off-the-door-skin-will-hit-whatever-part-that-was-sticking" method.  You know the one...   ... the one that doesn't work but seemed like a good idea at the time?<BR>Uugh... 2 years later, what a mess!  I took the panels off and wanted to put them right back on.  I mentally traced the problem to 80% sticking parts and 20% loose linkage.  #1) why did GM rivet bars in the way of parts that will eventually break? c'mon! bolts and screws are NOT that expensive.  #2) stupid little clips are a pain in the butt to fish out of the bottom of your door.  Especially when your hands are covered in grease... Hmm, how did that get there?  <BR>Once I got the actual lock and pivot piece out, I thoroughly cleaned both to pure metal with B-12 and could already tell a huge difference in the amount of force required to operate the parts.  A little tactfully placed lithium grease back in the metal to metal pieces and reinstall.  Viola!  they feel factory new!  As I was burning through  my can of Berryman's, I realized that no GM repair facility would take the time to do this... They would go find generic GM part XX locks and part XX actuators and charge me 2 hours labor per door.  It DID take a while to clean the locks, but I can't imagine a tech taking the time I did with this project.  I wonder how much I saved?<BR>Thanks for reading PFF members, I'm just bragging on my own resourcefulness and noting what a wonderful world it is when your doors work the first time!<P>"I love the smell of Berryman's in the morning... it smells like... victory..."||||reg||10
Z||000001||SKIDMARK||12-31-2001||06:27 PM||||AAAhhhh! there's nothing like a happy ending. I did the same thing except I locked the doors when I got to work and discovered while one door locks, the other unlocks. Then I remembered the basic DC motors unit in electrical science class and realized my mistake. After a week of debate I decided instead of just switching the wires around, I would disassemble the door and the motor and put the magnets back in the proper way. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
